WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.120
Every August, the best Dota 2 teams travel from around the world, seeking to

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.180
raise the coveted Aegis at the most prestigious competition in esports:

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.580
The International.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.420
Over its short but fabled history, The International has managed to

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.000
produce more than a few of Dota’s most unforgettable stories.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.560
(Casting)

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.640
But before more heroes and legends are added to the competition’s lore this summer

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.880
let’s look back, and relive the Most Iconic moments in The International’s history.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.800
The Fountain Hooks

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:47.560
(Casting)

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.240
Game-breaking. Brilliant. Hilarious. Cheating.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.740
No matter how you describe it,

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.200
Na`Vi’s Fountain Hooks from TI3 is still one of the most unorthodox strategies

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.660
to ever be used at an International.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:04.300
Only 15 minutes into game 3 of their upper bracket match against TongFu

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:07.160
Na`Vi found themselves with their backs against the wall.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.080
(Casting)

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.720
Sensing incoming defeat, Puppey and Dendi pulled out the ultimate cheese strategy – the Fountain Hook

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:24.920
(Casting)

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.120
The product of a unique interaction between Chen’s “Test of Faith” and Pudge’s “Meat Hook”,

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.880
the Fountain Hook created a long ride to certain death for

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.040
anyone unfortunate enough to be caught on the end of it.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.280
(Casting)

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.280
From there, things became more and more ridiculous

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.220
as Dendi landed hook, after hook,

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.200
after hook.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.120
(Crowd Cheering)

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.820
In the end, despite its absurdity, this cheese strategy was crucial for securing

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.200
Na`Vi’s comeback.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:08.360
(Casting)

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.240
While the interaction would later be patched out of the game,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:15.480
this iconic moment forever stands as a testament to not only Na`Vi’s inventiveness

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.920
but the near-endless possibilities of Dota.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:28.320
(Casting)

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.320
The Mega Creep Comeback

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.320
(Casting)

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.320
(Panel Talking)

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.040
In the six-year history of The International,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.220
no team had ever successfully overcome Mega Creeps to take the win.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.420
That is, until August 10th, 2016,

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:53.360
when EG met EHOME in an absolute slugfest of a match.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.280
(Panel Talking)

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.600
From minute one, both teams went blow-for-blow in teamfight after teamfight,

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:29.280
(Casting)

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.840
but EHOME slowly ground EG down and pushed forward,

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.420
eventually claiming all three ‘rax to get Mega Creeps.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:46.720
(Casting)

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.640
With the impossible challenge of overcoming Megas in front of them, EG didn’t waver,

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:56.360
putting up an unbelievable defence, somehow clawing their way back into the match.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:31.680
(Casting)

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.160
With the game well-past the hour mark, and Megas threatening their every move,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.720
EG took the momentum of their valiant hold and brought the fight to EHOME’s front door.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:05:00.400
(Casting)

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.740
As the crowd blew the roof off Key Arena,

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:09.360
EG cemented their legendary status with one of the greatest Dota 2 comebacks of all-time.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:13.600
The Deny

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.080
From The International 2013,

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.000
KYXY’s deny is one of the most infamous blunders of Dota’s history

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.480
and is iconic for all the wrong reasons.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:35.520
(Casting)

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:40.320
In the Lower Bracket finals, Orange eSports took on Na`Vi for the chance

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.100
to face Alliance in the Grand Finals.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:48.120
Orange came into the series hot, and jumped out to a strong lead in a deciding Game 3 against the favorites,

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:48.800
Na`Vi.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:06:01.440
(Casting)

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:07.340
Orange was in a commanding position, and opted to take Roshan to ensure the win.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.060
But that’s when disaster struck.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:16.400
(Casting)

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.800
As Roshan died, KYXY misclicked,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:24.320
destroying the crucial Aegis of the Immortal just before his team could pick it up.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:33.440
(Casting)

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:38.080
Without the Aegis in hand, Orange was unable to close out the game safely,

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.440
ultimately creating an opening for Na`Vi.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:48.080
(Casting)

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:52.000
Na`Vi capitalized on Orange’s misfortune and charged back into the game,

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:56.560
overcoming a 7 thousand gold deficit, knocking Orange out of the event.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:02.320
(Casting)

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.960
While KYXY is still a celebrated player, this moment will forever linger.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:12.300
Adding to the cruel iconic legacy of this misplay is the fact that, to this day,

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.460
Aegis denies are still called KYXY’s.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:22.800
(Casting)

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:26.480
The Six-Million Dollar Echo Slam

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:33.600
(Casting)

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.600
From the Grand Finals of TI5, The Six Million Dollar Echo Slam is not only one

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:42.840
of the nastiest two-man wombo combos
you'll ever witness,

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:46.140
it was the tournament-winning play for Evil Geniuses.

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:51.460
Up 2-1 in the Grand Final against CDEC-Gaming, Evil Geniuses – the hometown heroes

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.680
appeared to be on their way to victory in game four.

00:07:54.680 --> 00:08:03.360
(Casting)

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:10.320
Then, a glimmer of hope presented itself for CDEC: a catch onto SumaiL created an opening to take Roshan

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.720
and potentially reclaim the momentum.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.320
(Casting)

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:19.120
But, as quickly as the window opened for CDEC,

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.360
PPD and Universe slammed it shut even faster,

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.960
unleashing their ultimates onto their unwary opponents to devastating effect.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:44.080
(Casting)

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.920
Only 10 minutes after this game-defining play,

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.720
EG would hoist the Aegis as North America’s first ever champions.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:58.240
(Crowd chants: EG! EG! EG!)

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.440
s4's Million Dollar Dream Coils

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.400
From the epic Grand finals of TI3

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:10.220
s4’s Million Dollar Dream Coils are quite possibly the most clutch plays in Dota 2 history.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:15.820
TI3’s Grand Finals was the culmination of one of the game’s best rivalries:

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:19.480
Alliance vs. Na`Vi, the “El Clasico” of Dota.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:24.120
With the title on the line in a deciding Game 5,

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:29.680
both teams were digging deep. Alliance started slow out of the gate, giving Na`Vi early control.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:47.840
(Casting)

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.960
That’s when Alliance started to do what they do best: split-push.

00:09:52.960 --> 00:10:07.600
(Casting)

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.360
Na`Vi grouped up mid, attempting to force Alliance to defend.

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:15.600
When it became clear their strategy wasn’t working,

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.200
Na`Vi tried to bail out.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:25.040
But, s4 had something to say about that.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:28.720
(Casting)

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:35.200
With Na`Vi stalled in their recalls, Alliance took down two lanes of ‘rax, completely outmaneuvering their rival.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:59.520
(Casting)

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:03.040
As the game was coming down to the wire,

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.920
s4 once again came up absolutely massive.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:38.160
(Casting)

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.000
While there is plenty of debate as to which of the two moments is the true “Million Dollar Coil”,

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.180
there’s no doubt that both are spectacular.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:51.040
The plays catapulted s4 into the Dota pantheon and helped Alliance secure the Aegis

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.460
in one of the most epic finishes to any International.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.200
Dramatic, sensational and undeniably clutch

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:03.960
s4’s Million Dollar Dream Coils are the definition of an iconic Dota moment.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.960
BuLba and Clockwerk

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:18.960
(Casting)

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:21.920
Sometimes, one man can make all the difference in the world

00:12:21.950 --> 00:12:25.740
and during The International 2013, that man was BuLba,

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:29.560
who helped carry Team Liquid to one of the most dramatic upsets of all time.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.020
When Liquid met LGD-Gaming China in the Lower Bracket of TI3,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:38.900
everyone predicted that North America would once again fall short against a Chinese powerhouse.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.320
(Desk Talking)

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:47.120
However, in a now-legendary game, BuLba on Clockwerk went to work,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.240
picking LGD apart across the map and landing crucial hooks to give Liquid a chance.

00:12:52.240 --> 00:13:07.920
(Casting)

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:14.220
Thankfully for North American fans, Liquid did not waste the opportunity produced by BuLba’s heroics,

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:17.200
closing out the upset with a brilliant teamfight.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:36.720
(Casting)

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.760
With LD’s famous call resonating throughout the building, North America emerged the victors.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:56.160
(Casting)

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:01.200
The match remains iconic for not only BuLba’s ridiculously heroic individual performance,

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:07.860
but the significance of the win in the landscape of NA Dota.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.640
The Play

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:13.020
What can you say about The Play that hasn’t been said already?

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:17.880
From The International 2012, The Play is still to this day Dota’s most famous moment

00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.200
– a highlight you simply need to see to believe.

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:40.000
(Casting)

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:46.160
In a crucial Game 2 of their upper bracket match against Invictus Gaming, Na`Vi was in firm control.

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:51.140
With eyes on the Tier 2 bottom lane, Na`Vi gathered themselves and grouped for a push.

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:57.020
Sensing an opportunity to mount a comeback, iG didn’t hesitate.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:01.640
Wrapping around behind Na`Vi under cover of Smoke, iG’s plan worked perfectly,

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:06.620
catching Na`Vi off-guard and setting up what looked to be an absolutely devastating teamfight.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:35.680
(Casting)

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:42.660
However, LightofHeaven popped his BKB, landing a massive four-man black hole on the grouped-up iG,

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:44.500
completely reversing the trap.

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:48.640
At the same instant, Dendi Force Staffed himself out of iG’s attacks,

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:55.060
stealing Ravage and returning it in kind, leading Na`Vi to decimate iG in a clean wipe.

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:58.240
(Casting)

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.240
Na`Vi would go on to not only win the game, but the series as well,

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.340
advancing them into the Top 3 at TI2.

00:16:05.560 --> 00:16:09.640
While iG would earn their revenge in the Grand Finals to claim the Aegis for themselves,

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:13.300
it does little to tarnish what is Dota’s most iconic moment of all.

